.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY_CTX_SET_SCRYPT_N 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_scrypt_salt ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_N ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_r ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_p ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_maxmem_bytes
.Nd EVP_PKEY scrypt KDF support functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/kdf.h
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_scrypt_salt
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *salt"
.Fa "int saltlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_N
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "uint64_t N"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_r
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "uint64_t r"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_p
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "uint64_t p"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_maxmem_bytes
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "uint64_t maxmem"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions are used to set up the necessary data to use the scrypt
KDF. For more information on scrypt, see
.Xr EVP_KDF-SCRYPT 7 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_scrypt_salt
sets the
.Fa saltlen
bytes long salt value.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_N ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_r
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_p
configure the work factors N, r and p.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_scrypt_maxmem_bytes
sets how much RAM key derivation may maximally use, given in bytes.
If RAM is exceeded because the load factors are chosen too high, the key
derivation will fail.
.Sh STRING CTRLS
scrypt also supports string based control operations via
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_ctrl_str 3 .
Similarly, the
.Fa salt
can either be specified using the
.Sy type
parameter "salt" or in hex encoding by using the "hexsalt" parameter.
The work factors
.Fa N ,
.Fa r
and
.Fa p
as well as
.Sy maxmem_bytes
can be set by using the parameters "N", "r", "p" and "maxmem_bytes",
respectively.
.Sh NOTES
There is a newer generic API for KDFs,
.Xr EVP_KDF 3 ,
which is preferred over the EVP_PKEY method.
.Pp
The scrypt KDF also uses
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_pbe_pass 3
as well as the value from the string controls "pass" and "hexpass".
See
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_pbe_pass 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All these functions return 1 for success and 0 or a negative value for
failure.
In particular a return value of -2 indicates the operation is not
supported by the public key algorithm.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_KDF 3
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_ctrl_str 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_derive 3
.Sh HISTORY
All of the functions described here were converted from macros to
functions in OpenSSL 3.0.
